  "id": "Compound::DB01095 - Gene::3156",
  "verbalization": "Fluvastatin, Pravastatin, and Rosuvastatin are all statin drugs that inhibit Hmg-Coa reductase, a key enzyme in the cholesterol synthesis pathway. This inhibition leads to a reduction in cholesterol production, which is a well-documented mechanism of action for these compounds. The visible evidence suggests that Fluvastatin and its analogs cause muscle swelling or peripheral nerve palsy, which are adverse effects associated with statin use. These effects are mediated through the inhibition of Hmgcr, as indicated by the binding of Pravastatin and Rosuvastatin to the enzyme. The shared pathway from Fluvastatin to Hmgcr through the Statin class underscores the central role of Hmgcr in the pharmacological action of these drugs. The evidence supports a direct relationship between Fluvastatin and Hmgcr, mediated through the inhibition of Hmg-Coa reductase, and highlights the importance of this enzyme in the pathophysiology of statin-related adverse effects.",
